#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Alert when an agent lane on this machine has stopped: a dead Envoy role holder, or an agent
tmux pane left sitting at a shell.

Part one, the Envoy role check.

Why this exists: on 2026-10-27T05:52Z the Verified Linux owner session
(01a1798a-9911-7231-a1bb-cf29d7985273, role lead-verified-linux) stopped, wrote no error and
no exit record, and nothing noticed. Its Envoy role claim stayed registered, so messages to
notifications.role.lead-verified-linux looked deliverable; about 40 in-session subagent lanes
were lost, and a peer first learnt of it 11 minutes later from a "no live session" send error.

What one run does, mechanically, with no model call:

  1. GET /v1/sessions  -- every session Envoy lists as live, with its roles, host, dir and the
     timestamp of its last heartbeat.
  2. For each role whose holder session is on THIS machine (now, or remembered from an earlier
     run in the state file), establish what can be established about that holder from four
     sources: an `omp` process that names the session on its command line, in the file name of
     an open transcript, or in that transcript's own header; any process at all holding the
     session's transcript open; how long ago that transcript was last written; and Envoy's own
     registry entry and heartbeat (GET /v1/sessions, confirmed by GET /v1/registry/<id>, both
     of which only read).
  3. The watch calls no role route at all. GET /v1/roles/<role> is how Envoy releases a claim
     whose holder has left the session bucket (role_holder.go resolveLiveRoleHolder, still so on
     origin/main at d157fc868, checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z), and that holder is exactly the one
     this watch would ever ask about: a read that is a write. On 2026-10-29 the alert text named
     it, a reader ran it, and a live lane lost its claim two minutes after a false alert. Even
     once it is pure it buys little -- the claim says only that it is registered to the session
     the watch already cannot find -- so what the watch gives up with it is in `alert_text`.
  4. A holder is only ever UNSEEN, never dead. From a watcher's position "I cannot find it" is
     the strongest thing establishable: a loaded box stops heartbeating inside Envoy's 300 s TTL
     (a root session and 18 subagents measured silent for 17 minutes at load 233), and omp writes
     no exit record, so nothing here can prove a session ended. Every alert says which of the two
     it established, and `guard_verdict` refuses to send one that claims the holder is dead.
     A role alerts only when EVERY source is silent for the grace window (default 60 s); one
     silent source -- most often Envoy's registry, which drops live sessions under load -- is
     logged and never alerts, never opens an incident and never asks anyone to restart anything.

Alerts go over Envoy to agent sessions only, never to a person: the chief of staff, the INFRA
lead, the dead role's stream lead (the live `lead-*`/`stream-lead-*` holder in the same working
directory), and the lane's peers (the live holders of the team topics the dead session shared).
One alert per incident, one more when it clears, and one escalation to the chief of staff when
an incident is still open 10 minutes after its alert.

Part two, the tmux pane check (`check_panes`). After the 2026-10-27T13:56Z reboot, five of six
Verified Linux panes came back as a bash prompt: tmux-resurrect restored the layout but their omp
never started. No process died, so nothing above sees it, and only one of the six held a role. So
every pane of the default tmux server is read directly: an AGENT pane is one whose title omp set
(it starts "π "), or one this watch has seen omp running in, or one with an omp among the
descendants of its pane process. It is AT A SHELL when the pane process is a shell and is its own
terminal foreground process group -- nothing is running in it at all. After a 180 s grace that is
one alert naming the pane, the session it last ran, its handover files and the exact
`tmux send-keys` that restarts it, to the INFRA lead and the lane's stream lead; one clear when
omp runs there again or the pane goes away. The session is resolved from what this watch saw
running in the pane, else the transcript in the pane's directory whose own title the pane title
carries, else the `omp --resume` command tmux-resurrect saved for that pane's place.

The watcher does not execute that command. It cannot distinguish a failed session from one that
was deliberately `/pause`d before the process left its pane: omp keeps that state in process
memory and writes no durable session marker. It must only alert until omp persists a marker the
watch can check.

Scope: role holders, and panes. A session that holds no role and has no pane is not watched.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

# The two sessions that own this detector's findings: the chief of staff, who owns the incident
# class (dispatch://INFRA-602), and the INFRA lead, who owns this detector.
CHIEF_OF_STAFF = "01a13dca-e979-7264-af32-f654ef57a6f5"
INFRA_LEAD = "01a13c57-d826-7242-80fe-5f0670188b11"

# Where the state file goes. /mnt/nvme/sami/tmp is the agent devboxes' scratch on the big disk;
# an agent box without it keeps the state under ~/.local/state, which every XDG machine has.
# Measured 2026-10-30 on ossv-agent-open..open6, ossv-agent-git and ossv-agent-repo: /mnt/nvme
# there is a root-owned 755 directory on the root filesystem with no sami/tmp under it, so this
# watch crashed on its first run of every one of them with PermissionError. The test is
# writability, not existence: the path can be a directory this user still cannot write into, and
# `--state` cannot be the answer, since a non-default state file forces --dry-run and the watch
# would then be armed and permanently silent.
_NVME_STATE = Path("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/envoy-role-holder-watch/state.json")
DEFAULT_STATE = (
    _NVME_STATE
    if os.access("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp", os.W_OK)
    else Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".local/state")))
    / "envoy-role-holder-watch/state.json"
)
DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:9020"
SESSION_ROOT = Path.home() / ".omp/agent/sessions"

UUID_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}")
TEAM_TOPIC = "notifications.team."
# A lane's stream lead holds one of these roles in the lane's working directory.
LEAD_ROLE_RE = re.compile(r"^(stream-)?lead-")
# Files a restarted session is briefed from. Matched on the basename of an absolute .md path the
# session's own transcript names, and on the well-known names in its lane directories.
HANDOVER_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)(wake|handover|hand-off|handoff|state|brief|dod)")
HANDOVER_WELL_KNOWN = ("WAKE.md", "HANDOVER.md", "HANDOFF.md", "STATE.md")

# The two things this watch may say about a holder, and they are not symmetric.
#
# CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER is the strongest verdict any check here reaches: every source answered "not
# found", and not-found is not death.
#
# HOLDER_DEAD would need positive proof that the session ended -- an exit record, a reaped pid
# with a status, anything the session wrote on its way out. omp writes none: the 2026-10-27T05:52Z
# loss this watch exists for left no error and no exit record. So nothing in this file constructs
# it, `guard_verdict` refuses to send a message carrying it, and it is here only so that every
# alert can name which of the two it established.
HOLDER_DEAD = "the holder is dead"
CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER = "I cannot see this session"

# A transcript written this recently is a live session writing it. 120 s covers a model call and
# a long tool run; past it the file says nothing either way, which is why it is one silent source
# among four and not a verdict.
DEFAULT_WRITE_GRACE = 120.0
# A role whose holder has been unseen this long is dropped from the state file. Bookkeeping, not
# a judgement: the incident has already alerted and escalated, and nothing here re-reads it.
FORGET_AFTER = 86400.0

# THE MARKED PLACE FOR THE RELEASE CALL. Agent infra is adding an explicit release that records
# who released the claim, and the same change makes GET /v1/roles/<role> pure. Neither exists
# yet. Checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z, both ways:
#   - POST /v1/roles/release on the live listener answers 405.
#   - sjawhar/legion origin/main at d157fc868 has no roles/release handler anywhere, and
#     packages/envoy/cmd/listener/role_holder.go resolveLiveRoleHolder still calls
#     releaseExpiredRoleClaim for a holder that has left the session bucket -- which is the only
#     holder anyone reads this claim for. The lookup is still a write.
# When the route lands, this constant becomes the two sentences below and nothing else in
# `alert_text` moves. `{role}` and `{holder}` are filled in there, so a curl body's braces are
# doubled:
#   RELEASE_CALL = (
#       "RELEASE THE CLAIM, only once step 2 or 3 has established that no live lane holds it:"
#       " `curl -sS -X POST \"${{ENVOY_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:9020}}/v1/roles/release\""
#       " -H 'Content-Type: application/json'"
#       " -d '{{\"role\": \"{role}\", \"holder\": \"{holder}\","
#       " \"released_by\": \"<your session id or name>\"}}'`."
#       " Reading the claim changes nothing then:"
#       " `curl -sS \"${{ENVOY_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:9020}}/v1/roles/{role}\"`."
#   )
RELEASE_CALL = (
    "RELEASE THE CLAIM: there is no call for this yet, so this watch names none. Do NOT reach"
    " for the two that look like one. `curl ${{ENVOY_URL}}/v1/roles/{role}` releases a lapsed"
    " holder's claim as a side effect of answering (resolveLiveRoleHolder), and"
    " `curl -X DELETE ${{ENVOY_URL}}/v1/sessions/{holder}` unregisters a session that may be"
    " alive; together they are what cost role bet64-eta-arity its claim at 2026-10-29 09:22:31Z,"
    " two minutes after a false alert on a live lane. Tell the INFRA lead the role and holder"
    " and let the claim lapse on its own, or wait for agent infra's explicit release route,"
    " which this text will name as soon as it is deployed."
)


def log(message: str) -> None:
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    print(f"{stamp} {message}", flush=True)


def iso(epoch: float | None) -> str:
    if not epoch:
        return "unknown"
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


def human(seconds: float) -> str:
    seconds = int(seconds)
    if seconds < 90:
        return f"{seconds} s"
    minutes, rest = divmod(seconds, 60)
    if minutes < 90:
        return f"{minutes} min {rest} s"
    hours, minutes = divmod(minutes, 60)
    return f"{hours} h {minutes} min"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- Envoy transport


class Envoy:
    """The listener this machine's sessions use, resolved the way scripts/envoy resolves it."""

    def __init__(self, timeout: float = 8.0) -> None:
        path = os.environ.get("ENVOY_CONFIG_FILE") or str(
            Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".config")))
            / "legion/envoy.json"
        )
        config: dict = {}
        try:
            config = json.loads(Path(path).read_text()).get("envoy") or {}
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            config = {}
        if config.get("enabled") is False:
            raise SystemExit(f"{path} sets envoy.enabled to false: Envoy is off")
        self.url = (os.environ.get("ENVOY_URL") or config.get("url") or DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL).rstrip("/")
        self.timeout = timeout
        token = os.environ.get("ENVOY_TOKEN") or config.get("token") or ""
        if token.startswith("!"):
            # An envoy.json token may be a command; run it the way envoy-client does, and never
            # print either of its streams.
            try:
                done = subprocess.run(
                    ["/bin/sh", "-c", token[1:]],
                    capture_output=True,
                    text=True,
                    timeout=10,
                    stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                )
                token = done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else ""
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                token = ""
        self.token = token

    def request(self, method: str, path: str, body: dict | None = None) -> tuple[int, dict | list]:
        data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None
        request = urllib.request.Request(self.url + path, data=data, method=method)
        if data is not None:
            request.add_header("Content-Type", "application/json")
        if self.token:
            request.add_header("Authorization", f"Bearer {self.token}")
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=self.timeout) as answer:
                raw = answer.read().decode() or "{}"
                return answer.status, json.loads(raw)
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:
            raw = error.read().decode() or "{}"
            try:
                return error.code, json.loads(raw)
            except ValueError:
                return error.code, {"error": raw.strip()}
        except (urllib.error.URLError, socket.timeout, ValueError, OSError) as error:
            return 0, {"error": str(error)}

    def sessions(self) -> list[dict]:
        status, body = self.request("GET", "/v1/sessions")
        if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, list):
            raise RuntimeError(f"GET /v1/sessions: {status} {body}")
        return body

    # There is deliberately no role() here, for two reasons, and the first still holds today.
    # GET /v1/roles/<role> releases a claim whose holder has left the session bucket
    # (cmd/listener/role_holder.go resolveLiveRoleHolder -> releaseExpiredRoleClaim; still so on
    # sjawhar/legion origin/main at d157fc868, and the live listener answers 405 to the
    # POST /v1/roles/release that will replace it -- both checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z). That
    # holder is the only one this watch would ask about, so the read is a write. And even once
    # it is pure, its answer adds nothing this watch judges on: the claim's holder is the
    # session it has already failed to find.

    def registry(self, session_id: str) -> tuple[int, dict]:
        """Envoy's own registry entry for one session: a point read that writes nothing.

        `sessionEntryHandler` reads the session bucket and answers it (cmd/listener/api.go on
        sjawhar/legion origin/main at d157fc868, checked 2026-10-29 09:50Z). It shares that read
        with the role lookup but not the release the role lookup makes around it, so unlike the
        role lookup it is safe against a holder that has lapsed. 404 is Envoy saying it cannot
        see the session either; 200 says the /v1/sessions list this run read was stale.
        """
        status, body = self.request("GET", f"/v1/registry/{session_id}")
        return status, body if isinstance(body, dict) else {"error": str(body)}

    def send(self, session_id: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST",
            "/v1/messages/send",
            {"target_session": session_id, "message": message, "source": "envoy"},
        )
        if status != 200:
            log(f"  send to {session_id} failed: {status} {body}")
        return status == 200

    def publish(self, topic: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST", "/v1/messages/publish", {"topic": topic, "message": message, "source": "envoy"}
        )
        if status != 200:
            log(f"  publish to {topic} failed: {status} {body}")
        return status == 200


# --------------------------------------------------------------- this machine's omp processes


_HEADER_IDS: dict[tuple[int, int], str | None] = {}
_TRANSCRIPT_INDEX: tuple[dict[str, Path], list[Path]] | None = None


def session_id_in_header(path: str | Path) -> str | None:
    """The session id a transcript names in its own header, or None.

    A transcript's file name is not its identity, and neither is the command line that opened
    it. `omp --resume` takes a PATH as well as an id, and a lane that began as a subagent is
    resumed from a path named after the subagent (.../<stamp>_<parent id>/ArityLane.jsonl): no
    id on the command line, none in the file name, and a watch that reads only those two says a
    live session is gone. That is what happened at 2026-10-29 09:20:30Z on role bet64-eta-arity,
    whose holder was pid 724296, up 17,353 s, working with three subagents, and holding exactly
    such a file open.

    omp writes the id in a `{"type": "session", "id": ...}` record, which is not the first line:
    the first is the `title` record. So the first few records are read, not just one. Memoised on
    (device, inode), because a run reads the same transcript once per holding process.
    """
    try:
        info = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return None
    key = (info.st_dev, info.st_ino)
    if key in _HEADER_IDS:
        return _HEADER_IDS[key]
    found = None
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            for _ in range(8):
                line = handle.readline(65536)
                if not line:
                    break
                try:
                    record = json.loads(line)
                except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
                    continue
                if not isinstance(record, dict) or record.get("type") != "session":
                    continue
                match = UUID_RE.search(str(record.get("id") or ""))
                if match:
                    found = match.group(0)
                break
    except OSError:
        # A read that failed is not a file with no id in it. Caching it would make one transient
        # EMFILE or EACCES the answer for every later caller in this run.
        return None
    _HEADER_IDS[key] = found
    return found


def omp_sessions_by_pid() -> dict[str, int]:
    """Every session id an `omp` process on this machine holds, mapped to that pid.

    Three sources, in widening order: the command line (`omp --resume <id>`), the file name of
    an open transcript, and that transcript's own header. The header is what makes a resumed
    subagent lane visible; without it the process holds the session and nothing can tell.

    A pid here is positive evidence of life and nothing else. Its absence is not evidence of
    death: this reads only processes whose comm is `omp`, so a lane under a differently named
    parent is invisible to it, which is why `transcript_holders` reads the transcript's own
    openers before anything alerts.
    """
    held: dict[str, int] = {}
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        pid = int(entry)
        try:
            if Path(f"/proc/{pid}/comm").read_text().strip() != "omp":
                continue
            command = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline").read_bytes().decode("utf8", "replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        found = set(UUID_RE.findall(command))
        try:
            descriptors = os.listdir(f"/proc/{pid}/fd")
        except OSError:
            descriptors = []
        for descriptor in descriptors:
            try:
                target = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/{descriptor}")
            except OSError:
                continue
            if "/.omp/agent/sessions/" in target and target.endswith(".jsonl"):
                found.update(UUID_RE.findall(os.path.basename(target)))
                from_header = session_id_in_header(target)
                if from_header:
                    found.add(from_header)
        for session_id in found:
            held.setdefault(session_id, pid)
    return held


def transcript_holders(path: str | Path) -> list[int]:
    """Every pid on this machine with `path` open, whatever the process is called.

    `omp_sessions_by_pid` reads only processes whose comm is `omp`. This reads the file's own
    openers instead, by device and inode, so a lane running under a wrapper, a renamed binary or
    a box's init still counts as a holder. Run once per incident, not once per role per period:
    it stats every descriptor of every process this user can read.
    """
    try:
        info = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return []
    want = (info.st_dev, info.st_ino)
    holders: list[int] = []
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        directory = f"/proc/{entry}/fd"
        try:
            descriptors = os.listdir(directory)
        except OSError:
            continue
        for descriptor in descriptors:
            try:
                seen = os.stat(f"{directory}/{descriptor}")
            except OSError:
                continue
            if (seen.st_dev, seen.st_ino) == want:
                holders.append(int(entry))
                break
    return holders


def transcript_index() -> tuple[dict[str, Path], list[Path]]:
    """One walk of the session root per run: the id index, and the headers left to read.

    Returns ({session id taken from a file NAME: its newest transcript}, [transcripts whose
    name carries no id, newest first]).

    One walk, not one per role. `SESSION_ROOT.glob("**/*_<id>.jsonl")` costs 0.65 s over this
    box's 34,238 transcripts, and a run that called it once per role spent 19 s of its 25 s
    TimeoutStartSec on it (measured 2026-10-29 at load 77); a run systemd SIGTERMs is a watch
    that has stopped watching while its timer still looks healthy.
    """
    global _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX
    if _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX is not None:
        return _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX
    cutoff = time.time() - 7 * 86400
    by_id: dict[str, tuple[float, Path]] = {}
    unnamed: list[tuple[float, Path]] = []
    for root, _, names in os.walk(SESSION_ROOT, onerror=lambda _error: None):
        for name in names:
            if not name.endswith(".jsonl"):
                continue
            candidate = os.path.join(root, name)
            try:
                stamp = os.stat(candidate).st_mtime
            except OSError:
                continue
            match = UUID_RE.search(name)
            if match:
                best = by_id.get(match.group(0))
                if best is None or stamp > best[0]:
                    by_id[match.group(0)] = (stamp, Path(candidate))
            elif stamp >= cutoff:
                # Only these need their headers read, and only when the index misses.
                unnamed.append((stamp, Path(candidate)))
    unnamed.sort(key=lambda item: -item[0])
    _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX = (
        {session: path for session, (_, path) in by_id.items()},
        [path for _, path in unnamed],
    )
    return _TRANSCRIPT_INDEX


def transcript_of(session_id: str) -> Path | None:
    """The session's own transcript file, the newest one when a session was resumed.

    Two passes, for the same reason `session_id_in_header` exists: a file named `<stamp>_<id>`
    is found anywhere under the session root, including the subagent directories that
    `SESSION_ROOT.iterdir()` alone never descended into, and a file whose name carries no id at
    all (ArityLane.jsonl) is found by reading headers, newest first. That second pass is not
    cheap and not rare: 25,936 of this box's 34,272 transcripts carry no id in their name (every
    subagent's does not), and reading their headers costs about 1.5 s warm for the first miss in
    a run and ~0.3 s for each later one, from the memo. It runs only on a miss, and the walk
    behind it runs once per run, which is what keeps a whole period inside its 25 s budget.
    """
    if not UUID_RE.fullmatch(session_id or ""):
        return None
    by_id, unnamed = transcript_index()
    found = by_id.get(session_id)
    if found:
        return found
    for candidate in unnamed:
        if session_id_in_header(candidate) == session_id:
            return candidate
    return None


# --------------------------------------------------- what this run could establish about a holder


def holder_evidence(
    envoy: Envoy,
    holder: str,
    held: dict[str, int],
    live: dict[str, dict],
    write_grace: float,
    now: float,
) -> dict:
    """Every source this watch has on one holder, and what each one answered.

    Four sources, each of which can say "found" and none of which can say "gone":

      process    an `omp` process naming the session on its command line, in an open
                 transcript's file name, or in that transcript's header
      transcript how long ago the session's transcript was last written
      registry   Envoy's own entry and heartbeat, read with GET /v1/sessions and confirmed for
                 an absent holder with GET /v1/registry/<id>; never with GET /v1/roles/<role>,
                 which releases the claim of exactly the holder this watch cannot find
      openers    any process at all holding the session's transcript open. This one stats every
                 descriptor of every readable process (0.17 s here, 2,584 processes), so it is
                 read ONLY when the first three came back silent -- which is also exactly when
                 it can change the answer. A healthy role never pays for it, and an open
                 incident still pays for it every period, because it is the one source that can
                 see a lane running under a name this watch does not know and so the one that
                 can clear such an incident.

    `alive` is true when ANY source found it. The verdict when none did is CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER,
    and there is no path to HOLDER_DEAD: see that constant.
    """
    sources: list[tuple[str, bool, str]] = []

    pid = held.get(holder)
    sources.append(
        ("process", pid is not None,
         f"an omp process holds this session: pid {pid}" if pid
         else f"{CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} in any omp process on this machine (no command line names it,"
              " and no open transcript's name or header does)")
    )

    entry = live.get(holder)
    heartbeat = (entry or {}).get("last_seen")
    heartbeat = heartbeat / 1000 if isinstance(heartbeat, (int, float)) and heartbeat else None
    if entry is not None:
        # A registration is not a sighting; a recent HEARTBEAT is, because a process that has
        # stopped cannot send one. Envoy keeps an entry for its whole 300 s TTL after the last
        # beat, and that stale window is exactly the one the 2026-10-27T05:52Z loss hid in: the
        # role looked deliverable for five minutes after the session stopped. Measured on this
        # box, 610 live sessions beat at a median age of 43 s and p90 86 s, so the same
        # --write-grace window (120 s) separates a beating session from a stale entry, and a
        # session that beats more slowly than that is covered by the other three sources.
        fresh = heartbeat is not None and now - heartbeat <= write_grace
        registry_state = "live" if fresh else "stale"
        sources.append(
            ("registry", fresh,
             f"Envoy heard from this session {human(now - heartbeat)} ago"
             if fresh else
             (f"Envoy still lists this session, but its last heartbeat was"
              f" {human(now - heartbeat)} ago, outside the {int(write_grace)} s window: a"
              " registration outlives its holder by up to the 300 s TTL, so the entry alone is"
              " not a sighting"
              if heartbeat else
              "Envoy lists this session but records no heartbeat time, so the entry is not a"
              " sighting"))
        )
    else:
        status, body = envoy.registry(holder)
        if status == 404:
            registry_state = "absent"
            sources.append(
                ("registry", False,
                 f"{CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} in Envoy's registry: absent from GET /v1/sessions, and"
                 " GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered 404. Envoy drops a live session that stops"
                 " heartbeating inside its 300 s TTL, which a loaded box does, so this says"
                 " nothing about the process")
            )
        elif status == 200:
            beat = body.get("last_seen")
            beat = beat / 1000 if isinstance(beat, (int, float)) and beat else None
            heartbeat = beat
            fresh = beat is not None and now - beat <= write_grace
            registry_state = "live" if fresh else "stale"
            sources.append(
                ("registry", fresh,
                 "Envoy's registry holds this session and heard from it"
                 f" {human(now - beat)} ago (GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered 200); the"
                 " /v1/sessions list this run read did not hold it"
                 if fresh else
                 "Envoy's registry holds this session (GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered 200)"
                 f" but its last heartbeat was {human(now - beat) if beat else 'never recorded'},"
                 " which is not a sighting")
            )
        else:
            registry_state = "unreadable"
            sources.append(
                ("registry", False,
                 f"Envoy's registry could not be read: GET /v1/registry/<holder> answered"
                 f" {status} {body.get('error') or ''}".strip())
            )

    transcript = transcript_of(holder)
    last_write = None
    if transcript:
        try:
            last_write = transcript.stat().st_mtime
        except OSError:
            last_write = None
    if last_write and now - last_write <= write_grace:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", True,
             f"its transcript was written {human(now - last_write)} ago, inside the"
             f" {int(write_grace)} s window: something is still writing this session")
        )
    elif last_write:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", False,
             f"its transcript was last written {human(now - last_write)} ago ({iso(last_write)}),"
             f" outside the {int(write_grace)} s window -- which a session waiting on a model or"
             " a long tool call also looks like")
        )
    else:
        sources.append(
            ("transcript", False,
             f"{CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} in any transcript under {SESSION_ROOT}, by file name or header")
        )

    openers: list[int] = []
    if not any(seen for _, seen, _ in sources):
        if transcript:
            openers = transcript_holders(transcript)
            sources.append(
                ("openers", bool(openers),
                 f"its transcript is open in pid(s) {', '.join(str(p) for p in openers)}"
                 if openers
                 else "no process THIS USER CAN READ holds its transcript open, whatever the"
                      " process is called -- a lane inside an agent box has a uid-shifted"
                      " /proc and is invisible here"
                 )
            )
        else:
            # Said out loud rather than left out: three rows and four sources would read as a
            # source that answered nothing, when in fact there was no file to look at.
            sources.append(
                ("openers", False,
                 "not looked at: there is no transcript to find openers of")
            )

    found = next((sentence for _, seen, sentence in sources if seen), "")
    return {
        "alive": bool(found),
        "found": found,
        "pid": pid or (openers[0] if openers else None),
        "sources": sources,
        "registry": registry_state,
        "heartbeat": heartbeat,
        "transcript": str(transcript or ""),
        "last_write": last_write,
        "verdict": found or CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER,
    }


def guard_verdict(text: str) -> str:
    """Refuse to send a message that claims a holder is dead.

    Nothing in this file establishes that, so a message that says it is a defect, not a finding.
    Cheap, and it is the one rule the 2026-10-29 incident turned on.
    """
    if HOLDER_DEAD in text:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"refusing to send a message that claims {HOLDER_DEAD!r}: this watch cannot"
            " establish that"
        )
    return text


def handover_files(session_id: str, directory: str) -> list[str]:
    """The files a restarted session is briefed from, newest first.

    Two mechanical sources: the absolute .md paths the session's own transcript names whose
    basename reads like a handover, and the well-known names in the session's working directory
    and its lane scratch directories. Runs only when an alert is about to be sent.
    """
    found: dict[str, float] = {}
    transcript = transcript_of(session_id)
    if transcript:
        path_re = re.compile(r"/[^\s\"'\\]{3,180}?\.md")
        try:
            size = transcript.stat().st_size
            with transcript.open("rb") as handle:
                chunks = [handle.read(2_000_000)]
                if size > 4_000_000:
                    handle.seek(size - 2_000_000)
                    chunks.append(handle.read())
            for chunk in chunks:
                for match in path_re.findall(chunk.decode("utf8", "replace")):
                    if HANDOVER_NAME_RE.search(os.path.basename(match)):
                        try:
                            found[match] = os.path.getmtime(match)
                        except OSError:
                            continue
        except OSError:
            pass
    lane = os.path.basename(directory.rstrip("/")) if directory else ""
    roots = [directory] if directory else []
    if lane:
        roots += [f"/mnt/nvme/sami/{lane}", f"/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/{lane}"]
    for root in roots:
        for name in HANDOVER_WELL_KNOWN:
            candidate = os.path.join(root, name)
            try:
                found[candidate] = os.path.getmtime(candidate)
            except OSError:
                continue
    return [path for path, _ in sorted(found.items(), key=lambda item: -item[1])][:3]


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the decision


class State(object):
    """The one file this watch owns: the role roster and the open incidents.

    It is opened under an exclusive, non-blocking lock, so a hand run and the timer can never
    both decide that an incident is new -- the loser skips its period. `commit` writes the whole
    file and fsyncs it, and every alert commits before it sends, so the once-per-incident record
    is on disk before the message exists.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: Path) -> None:
        self.path = path
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        self.handle = open(path, "a+")
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            self.locked = True
        except OSError:
            self.locked = False
            self.data = {}
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        try:
            self.data = json.loads(self.handle.read() or "{}")
        except ValueError:
            self.data = {}

    def commit(self, options: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
        if options.dry_run and not options.write_state:
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        self.handle.truncate()
        json.dump(self.data, self.handle, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        self.handle.flush()
        os.fsync(self.handle.fileno())

    def close(self) -> None:
        try:
            self.handle.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


def recipients(role: str, record: dict, sessions: list[dict], holder: str) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str], list[str]]:
    """Who hears about `role`: named sessions, the stream lead, the lane's peers, and topics.

    Returns (targets, topics, notes). A target is (session id, why). Envoy keeps no record of
    who recently messaged a role, so the lane's peers are the live holders of the team topics
    the dead session shared -- the sessions whose messages to this role would vanish.
    """
    notes: list[str] = []
    targets = [(CHIEF_OF_STAFF, "chief of staff"), (INFRA_LEAD, "INFRA lead")]
    directory = record.get("dir") or ""
    leads = [
        session
        for session in sessions
        if session.get("session_id") != holder
        and (session.get("dir") or "") == directory
        and any(LEAD_ROLE_RE.match(name) for name in session.get("roles") or [])
    ]
    leads.sort(key=lambda s: 0 if any(n.startswith("stream-lead-") for n in s.get("roles") or []) else 1)
    if leads:
        lead = leads[0]
        lead_role = next(iter(sorted(lead.get("roles") or [])), "?")
        targets.append((lead["session_id"], f"stream lead ({lead_role})"))
    else:
        notes.append(
            f"No stream lead resolved: no live lead-* or stream-lead-* role holder works in {directory or '(no dir)'}."
        )

    team_topics = [topic for topic in record.get("topics") or [] if topic.startswith(TEAM_TOPIC)]
    team_bases = {topic[:-2] if topic.endswith(".>") else topic for topic in team_topics}
    peers = []
    for session in sessions:
        if session.get("session_id") in {holder} | {t[0] for t in targets}:
            continue
        shared = team_bases & {
            topic[:-2] if topic.endswith(".>") else topic for topic in session.get("topics") or []
        }
        if shared:
            peers.append((session["session_id"], f"lane peer on {sorted(shared)[0]}"))
    if team_bases:
        notes.append(
            "Peers were told on " + ", ".join(sorted(team_bases)) + "."
        )
    else:
        notes.append(
            "Envoy keeps no record of who recently messaged this role, and the unseen session "
            "shared no team topic, so its peers could not be named: only the sessions above were told."
        )
    return targets + peers[:6], sorted(team_bases), notes


def alert_text(role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, now: float, kind: str) -> str:
    """The alert for one incident. Every value it names was fixed when the incident opened.

    Two rules this text exists to keep. It establishes that the watch cannot see the holder, and
    says so in those words; it never says the holder is dead, which no check here can show. And
    it names no call that changes anything: not `omp --resume <id>` in a new pane, which puts a
    second process on a transcript the first may still hold (INFRA-681), and not the role lookup
    or the session delete, each of which acts on a session that may be alive. What it asks for
    instead is what caught the 2026-10-29 false alert in two minutes: look at the pane, and ask
    the session.
    """
    record = incident
    unseen_for = human(now - (record.get("opened_at") or now))
    transcript = record.get("transcript") or "(no transcript file found)"
    files = record.get("handover") or []
    handover = (
        "brief it from " + ", ".join(files)
        if files
        else "no handover file found: brief it from the tail of its transcript"
    )
    pane = record.get("pane") or ""
    look = (
        f"Look at pane {pane} -- this watch last saw this session running there."
        if pane
        else "Find its pane: `tmux list-panes -a -F '#{pane_id} #{pane_title} #{pane_current_path}'`"
        f" and look for its title or its directory, {record.get('dir') or '(no dir recorded)'}."
    )
    lines = [
        f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN ({kind}): nothing this watch can read has seen the holder of"
        f" role {role} for {unseen_for}, since {iso(record.get('opened_at'))}.",
        f"Holder: {holder} \"{record.get('title') or '-'}\" in {record.get('dir') or '-'} on"
        f" {record.get('host') or '-'}.",
        f"ESTABLISHED: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER}. NOT established: that the holder has stopped. No check"
        " this watch makes can show that, so treat every line below as a search, not a verdict.",
        "What each source answered when the incident opened:",
    ]
    lines += [f"  - {name}: {sentence}" for name, _, sentence in record.get("sources") or []]
    lines.append(
        f"While this holds, a message to notifications.role.{role} may reach nobody, and the"
        " claim may still be registered to this holder. This watch does not read the claim, and"
        " nor should you while the lane may be alive: GET /v1/roles/<role> releases a lapsed"
        " holder's claim as it answers (checked against the deployed listener on"
        " 2026-10-29). Steps 1 and 2 below settle the same question without touching anything."
    )
    lines += [
        "Do, in this order, and stop as soon as one answers:",
        f"  1. {look}",
        "  2. Ask the session. Type a question into that pane, or `envoy send"
        f" {holder} \"are you alive?\"`. A session that answers is alive and this alert is wrong:"
        " say so on the incident and nothing else needs doing. That is how the 2026-10-29"
        " 09:20:30Z false alert on bet64-eta-arity was caught, two minutes in.",
        f"  3. Only once a person has looked and found no live lane: read the tail of {transcript},"
        f" and restart the lane IN ITS OWN PANE, never a new one -- two omp processes on one"
        f" transcript is the INFRA-681 double loop. {handover}.",
        f"  4. {RELEASE_CALL.format(role=role, holder=holder)}",
    ]
    lines += record.get("notes") or []
    lines.append(
        "From envoy-role-holder-watch (~/.dotfiles/scripts/envoy-role-holder-watch) on "
        f"{socket.gethostname()}, a systemd --user timer every 30 s."
    )
    return guard_verdict("\n".join(lines))


def clear_text(role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, cause: str, now: float) -> str:
    opened = incident.get("opened_at") or now
    return guard_verdict(
        f"ENVOY ROLE HOLDER UNSEEN, CLEARED: role {role} has a holder this watch can see again. "
        f"What cleared it: {cause}. "
        f"The unseen holder was {holder}; the incident opened at {iso(opened)}, cleared at "
        f"{iso(now)}, and lasted {human(now - opened)}. "
        "No action is needed. From envoy-role-holder-watch on " + socket.gethostname() + "."
    )


def send_clear(
    envoy: Envoy, options: argparse.Namespace, role: str, incident: dict, holder: str, cause: str, now: float
) -> bool:
    """Tell everyone who heard the alert that the role has a holder again. One per incident."""
    if not incident.get("alerted_at") or incident.get("cleared_at"):
        return False
    incident["cleared_at"] = now
    targets = incident.get("told") or [CHIEF_OF_STAFF, INFRA_LEAD]
    text = clear_text(role, incident, holder, cause, now)
    for target in targets:
        if not options.dry_run:
            envoy.send(target, text)
    log(f"{role}: CLEARED ({cause}); told {len(targets)} sessions")
    if options.print_alert:
        print(text)
    return True

# ------------------------------------------------------------- agent panes left at a shell
#
# Why: after the 2026-10-27 13:56Z reboot, five of six Verified Linux panes came back as a bash
# prompt. Their omp never started, so no process vanished, and only one held an Envoy role, so the
# role check above saw one of six. This check reads the panes themselves.

SHELLS = {"bash", "sh", "zsh", "fish", "dash"}
RESURRECT_DIR = Path.home() / ".local/share/tmux/resurrect"
PANE_FIELDS = (
    "pid", "start_time", "pane_id", "session_name", "window_index", "window_name",
    "pane_index", "pane_current_command", "pane_pid", "pane_current_path", "pane_title",
)


def tmux_panes() -> list[dict] | None:
    """Every pane of the default tmux server, once each (grouped sessions list a pane per group
    member). None when tmux could not be read; [] when no server runs."""
    fmt = "\t".join("#{" + field + "}" for field in PANE_FIELDS)
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", fmt],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        log(f"panes: tmux list-panes failed: {error}")
        return None
    if done.returncode != 0:
        if "no server running" in done.stderr or "error connecting" in done.stderr:
            # Said out loud: a watch that silently sees no pane is the failure this check exists
            # to catch. The socket is $TMUX_TMPDIR/tmux-<uid>/default, which .bashrc sets and the
            # service unit repeats, since a systemd service sources no shell.
            log(f"panes: {done.stderr.strip()} (TMUX_TMPDIR={os.environ.get('TMUX_TMPDIR', '(unset)')})")
            return []
        log(f"panes: tmux list-panes exited {done.returncode}: {done.stderr.strip()}")
        return None
    panes: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        values = line.split("\t", len(PANE_FIELDS) - 1)
        if len(values) == len(PANE_FIELDS):
            pane = dict(zip(PANE_FIELDS, values))
            panes.setdefault(pane["pane_id"], pane)
    return list(panes.values())


def process_table() -> tuple[dict[int, list[int]], dict[int, tuple[str, int]]]:
    """children by parent pid, and (comm, foreground process group of its tty) by pid."""
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    info: dict[int, tuple[str, int]] = {}
    for entry in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not entry.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            raw = Path(f"/proc/{entry}/stat").read_text()
        except OSError:
            continue
        comm = raw[raw.index("(") + 1 : raw.rindex(")")]
        fields = raw[raw.rindex(")") + 2 :].split()
        pid, ppid, tpgid = int(entry), int(fields[1]), int(fields[5])
        children.setdefault(ppid, []).append(pid)
        info[pid] = (comm, tpgid)
    return children, info


def title_text(title: str) -> str:
    """An omp pane title without its `π` mark and spinner frame: `π ⠼ Fix X` -> `Fix X`."""
    parts = title.split(" ", 2)
    return parts[2].strip() if len(parts) == 3 and parts[0] == "π" else title.strip()


def session_dir_of(cwd: str) -> Path:
    """omp's transcript directory for a working directory (-src-x under $HOME, --abs-path-- else)."""
    home = str(Path.home())
    if cwd == home or cwd.startswith(home + "/"):
        return SESSION_ROOT / ("-" + cwd[len(home) + 1 :].replace("/", "-"))
    return SESSION_ROOT / ("--" + cwd.strip("/").replace("/", "-") + "--")


def transcript_by_title(cwd: str, text: str, live: dict[str, int]) -> tuple[str, Path] | None:
    """The newest top-level transcript in the pane's directory whose title (its first line) the
    pane title carries, and whose session no live process holds."""
    best: tuple[float, str, Path] | None = None
    try:
        candidates = list(session_dir_of(cwd).glob("*.jsonl"))
    except OSError:
        return None
    for candidate in candidates:
        try:
            with candidate.open("rb") as handle:
                first = json.loads(handle.readline(4096) or b"{}")
            stamp = candidate.stat().st_mtime
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        title = (first.get("title") or "").strip() if isinstance(first, dict) else ""
        ids = UUID_RE.findall(candidate.name)
        if not title or not ids or title not in text or ids[-1] in live:
            continue
        if best is None or stamp > best[0]:
            best = (stamp, ids[-1], candidate)
    return (best[1], best[2]) if best else None


def resurrect_command(pane: dict, text: str, limit: int = 60) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """The newest tmux-resurrect save in which this pane's place (session, window, pane index),
    under the same title, ran `omp --resume`: (its command, the save file)."""
    try:
        saves = sorted(RESURRECT_DIR.glob("tmux_resurrect_*.txt"), reverse=True)[:limit]
    except OSError:
        return None
    place = (pane["session_name"], pane["window_index"], pane["pane_index"])
    for save in saves:
        try:
            lines = save.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines()
        except OSError:
            continue
        for line in lines:
            fields = line.split("\t")
            if len(fields) < 11 or fields[0] != "pane" or (fields[1], fields[2], fields[5]) != place:
                continue
            command = fields[10].lstrip(":")
            if "--resume" in command and title_text(fields[6]) == text:
                words = command.split()
                if words and words[0].endswith("/omp"):
                    words[0] = "omp"
                return " ".join(words), str(save)
    return None


def resume_target(command: str) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """The session and transcript an `omp … --resume <value>` command actually resumes.

    `--resume` takes an id OR a path, and a path is where the 2026-10-29 blind spot lives a
    second time: `omp --resume .../<stamp>_<parent id>/ArityLane.jsonl` carries the PARENT's
    uuid in its directory name and none of its own, so taking the last uuid anywhere in the
    command names a different, possibly live session. The pane alert then checks the wrong
    transcript for holders, finds none, and tells a reader to start a second omp on a lane that
    is running. Read the path's header instead; fall back to a bare uuid argument.
    """
    words = command.split()
    value = ""
    for index, word in enumerate(words):
        if word == "--resume" and index + 1 < len(words):
            value = words[index + 1]
            break
        if word.startswith("--resume="):
            value = word.split("=", 1)[1]
            break
    value = value.strip("'\"")
    if not value:
        return None, None
    if UUID_RE.fullmatch(value):
        return value, None
    # Anything else is a path. Its header is the ONLY thing that names its session: the uuids
    # in the path itself are its parent's and its grandparent's, and naming one of those is how
    # the pane alert ends up checking a live lane's sibling for holders. A header this watch
    # cannot read leaves the session unresolved, which the alert says out loud, rather than
    # guessed from the path.
    return session_id_in_header(value), value


def pane_alert_text(incident: dict, now: float) -> str:
    where = incident["where"]
    session = incident.get("session")
    lines = [
        f"AGENT PANE AT SHELL: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']} ({where}, window"
        f" \"{incident['window_name']}\") has sat at a {incident['shell']} prompt with no omp in it for"
        f" {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}, first seen at {iso(incident['opened_at'])}.",
        f"Evidence: the pane's foreground process group is its own shell (pid {incident['shell_pid']},"
        f" /proc/<pid>/stat tpgid), no omp process descends from it, and its title still reads"
        f" \"{incident['title']}\" (omp sets that `π` title; a plain shell never does). cwd {incident['cwd']}.",
    ]
    last_write = incident.get("last_write")
    # The one fact that separates a planned wind-down from a crash gets a line of its own: OSS
    # Verify read 00:51:59Z off the first of these alerts and recognised its own planned stop.
    lines.append(
        f"LAST TRANSCRIPT WRITE: {iso(last_write)}"
        + (f" ({human(now - last_write)} before this alert)." if last_write
           else " -- no transcript resolved, so there is no last write to compare.")
        + " A write at the moment the lane was wound down reads as a planned stop. One that"
        " stops mid-work does NOT establish that it died -- a session can stop writing while it"
        " waits on a model or a long tool call, and this watch cannot tell the two apart."
    )
    if session:
        lines.append(f"Last session: {session} ({incident['session_source']}).")
        if incident.get("transcript"):
            lines.append(f"Transcript: {incident['transcript']}.")
    else:
        lines.append(
            "Last session: NOT RESOLVED -- this watch never saw omp run here, no transcript in"
            f" {session_dir_of(incident['cwd'])} carries the pane's title, and no tmux-resurrect save"
            " records a resume command for this place. Read the newest transcripts there."
        )
    files = incident.get("handover") or []
    brief = "brief it from " + ", ".join(files) if files else "no handover file found: brief it from the tail of its transcript"
    lines.append(
        f"ESTABLISHED: this pane is at a shell. NOT established: that its session has stopped --"
        " the session may be running in another pane, in a box, or under another process."
    )
    if incident.get("restart"):
        target = incident.get("transcript") or ""
        check = (
            f"check first that no process holds its transcript"
            f" (`ls -l /proc/*/fd 2>/dev/null | grep -F {shlex.quote(target)}`)"
            if target
            else "this watch resolved no transcript for it, so there is nothing to check for"
            " holders: find the lane before you start a second one"
        )
        lines.append(
            f"Do: {check}, and only then restart it IN THAT PANE, never a new one:"
            f" `tmux send-keys -t {incident['pane_id']} {shlex.quote(incident['restart'])} Enter`."
            f" Then {brief}."
        )
        lines.append(
            "Automatic restart: NOT RUN. omp does not persist `/pause` state after its process"
            " exits, so this watch cannot safely tell a failed session from a deliberately paused one."
        )
    else:
        lines.append(f"Do: start the lane again in that pane, never a new one, and {brief}.")
    lines += incident.get("notes") or []
    told = incident.get("told_why") or []
    if told:
        lines.append("Told: " + "; ".join(told) + ".")
    lines.append(
        "One alert per pane per incident, one clear when omp runs there again. From"
        f" envoy-role-holder-watch (~/.dotfiles/scripts/envoy-role-holder-watch) on {socket.gethostname()}."
    )
    return guard_verdict("\n".join(lines))


def pane_recipients(directory: str, session: str | None, sessions: list[dict]) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str]]:
    targets = [(INFRA_LEAD, "INFRA lead")]
    leads = [
        s for s in sessions
        if s.get("session_id") not in {session, INFRA_LEAD}
        and (s.get("dir") or "") == directory
        and any(LEAD_ROLE_RE.match(name) for name in s.get("roles") or [])
    ]
    leads.sort(key=lambda s: 0 if any(n.startswith("stream-lead-") for n in s.get("roles") or []) else 1)
    if leads:
        role = next(iter(sorted(leads[0].get("roles") or [])), "?")
        targets.append((leads[0]["session_id"], f"stream lead ({role})"))
        return targets, []
    return targets, [f"No stream lead resolved: no live lead-* or stream-lead-* role holder works in {directory}."]


def check_panes(options: argparse.Namespace, state: State, envoy: Envoy, sessions: list[dict], held: dict[str, int], now: float) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]:
    """(agent panes, agent panes at a shell, alerts, clears) for the default tmux server."""
    panes = tmux_panes()
    if panes is None:
        return 0, 0, 0, 0
    memory: dict = state.data.setdefault("panes", {})
    incidents: dict = state.data.setdefault("pane_incidents", {})
    if not panes and (memory or incidents):
        # An empty pane list is the other absence that is not evidence. `tmux list-panes`
        # answers "no server running" both when the server really went away and when this
        # process cannot reach its socket -- a wrong TMUX_TMPDIR, a mise/system tmux version
        # mismatch. Clearing on it would send "the pane no longer exists" for every open
        # incident and forget every pane this watch has learnt, on a run that read nothing.
        # A run that saw panes before and sees none now holds instead, and says so.
        log(
            f"panes: no pane at all, but this watch remembers {len(memory)} and holds"
            f" {len(incidents)} open incident(s); holding rather than clearing on an empty read"
            f" (TMUX_TMPDIR={os.environ.get('TMUX_TMPDIR', '(unset)')})"
        )
        return 0, 0, 0, 0
    children, info = process_table()
    pid_session = {pid: session for session, pid in held.items()}
    seen_keys = set()
    agents = at_shell = alerts = clears = 0

    def clear(key: str, incident: dict, cause: str) -> None:
        nonlocal clears
        if incident.get("alerted_at") and not incident.get("cleared_at"):
            incident["cleared_at"] = now
            state.commit(options)
            text = guard_verdict(
                f"AGENT PANE AT SHELL CLEARED: tmux pane {incident['pane_id']} ({incident['where']})."
                f" What cleared it: {cause}. The incident opened at {iso(incident['opened_at'])} and"
                f" lasted {human(now - incident['opened_at'])}. No action is needed. From"
                f" envoy-role-holder-watch on {socket.gethostname()}."
            )
            for target in incident.get("told") or [INFRA_LEAD]:
                if not options.dry_run:
                    envoy.send(target, text)
            clears += 1
            log(f"pane {incident['pane_id']} {incident['where']}: CLEARED ({cause})")
            if options.print_alert:
                print(text)
        incidents.pop(key, None)

    for pane in panes:
        key = f"{pane['pid']}.{pane['start_time']}:{pane['pane_id']}"
        seen_keys.add(key)
        where = f"{pane['session_name']}:{pane['window_index']}.{pane['pane_index']}"
        pane_pid = int(pane["pane_pid"] or 0)
        stack, omp_pids = [pane_pid], []
        while stack:
            pid = stack.pop()
            if info.get(pid, ("", 0))[0] == "omp":
                omp_pids.append(pid)
            stack.extend(children.get(pid, []))
        remembered = memory.get(key)
        if not (pane["pane_title"].startswith("π ") or remembered or omp_pids):
            continue
        agents += 1
        incident = incidents.get(key)
        if omp_pids:
            session = next((pid_session[p] for p in omp_pids if p in pid_session), None)
            record = memory.setdefault(key, {})
            record.update({"where": where, "title": pane["pane_title"], "cwd": pane["pane_current_path"], "seen_at": now})
            if session:
                record["session"] = session
            if incident:
                clear(key, incident, f"omp (pid {omp_pids[0]}) runs in the pane again"
                      + (f", session {session}" if session else ""))
            continue
        comm, tpgid = info.get(pane_pid, ("", 0))
        if not (comm in SHELLS and tpgid == pane_pid):
            log(f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: agent pane runs {pane['pane_current_command']}, not omp and not a bare shell; holding")
            continue
        at_shell += 1
        text = title_text(pane["pane_title"])
        # Resolve the session this pane last ran: what this watch saw running here, else the
        # transcript in its directory titled as the pane is, else the newest resurrect save.
        if incident is None or "session_source" not in incident:
            session = restart = transcript = None
            source = ""
            if remembered and remembered.get("session"):
                session = remembered["session"]
                source = f"seen running in this pane by this watch at {iso(remembered.get('seen_at'))}"
            else:
                found = transcript_by_title(pane["pane_current_path"], text, held)
                if found:
                    session, transcript = found[0], str(found[1])
                    source = f"the transcript in the pane's directory whose title is in the pane title, {found[1].name}"
                else:
                    saved = resurrect_command(pane, text)
                    if saved:
                        restart = saved[0]
                        session, transcript = resume_target(saved[0])
                        source = (
                            f"the resume command tmux-resurrect saved for {where} in {saved[1]}"
                            + ("" if session else
                               " -- which names a path whose header this watch could not read,"
                               " so the session is NOT resolved")
                        )
            incident = incidents.setdefault(key, {"opened_at": now})
            transcript = transcript or (str(transcript_of(session) or "") if session and UUID_RE.fullmatch(session) else "")
            if transcript and not os.path.exists(transcript):
                # A path a resume command named that is not there is not a transcript; keeping
                # it would put a `grep -F <path>` in the alert that can never match and reads
                # to a person as "nothing holds it".
                transcript = ""
            incident.update({
                "pane_id": pane["pane_id"], "where": where, "window_name": pane["window_name"],
                "title": pane["pane_title"], "cwd": pane["pane_current_path"], "shell": comm,
                "shell_pid": pane_pid, "session": session, "session_source": source,
                "transcript": transcript,
                "restart": restart or (f"omp --resume {session}" if session else ""),
            })
            try:
                incident["last_write"] = os.path.getmtime(transcript) if transcript else None
            except OSError:
                incident["last_write"] = None
        # EVERY period, not only the one that opened the incident. The pane being at a shell is
        # a fact about the pane; whether its session is running somewhere else is a fact about
        # the session, and that one can change between the open and the 180 s alert. Checking it
        # once meant an alert could name a session that was live at the moment it was sent, and
        # hand a reader the send-keys that starts a second omp on its transcript -- the
        # INFRA-681 double loop the same alert warns about.
        session = incident.get("session")
        if session and session in held:
            log(
                f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell, but its session {session} runs in"
                f" pid {held[session]} elsewhere; not an incident"
            )
            clear(key, incident, f"its session {session} runs in pid {held[session]} elsewhere")
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            state.commit(options)
            continue
        open_for = now - incident["opened_at"]
        if open_for < options.pane_grace:
            log(f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell for {int(open_for)} s (< {int(options.pane_grace)} s grace), holding")
            state.commit(options)
            continue
        if incident.get("alerted_at"):
            log(f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: at a shell for {human(open_for)}, already alerted at {iso(incident['alerted_at'])}")
            continue
        session = incident.get("session")
        directory = pane["pane_current_path"]
        if incident.get("transcript"):
            try:
                with open(incident["transcript"], "rb") as handle:
                    for _ in range(3):
                        entry = json.loads(handle.readline(65536) or b"{}")
                        if entry.get("type") == "session":
                            directory = entry.get("cwd") or directory
                            break
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                pass
        incident["handover"] = handover_files(session if session and UUID_RE.fullmatch(session) else "-", directory)
        targets, notes = pane_recipients(directory, session, sessions)
        incident["notes"] = notes
        # Named in the alert, so a reader knows who else saw it and under which role.
        incident["told_why"] = [f"{why} {target}" for target, why in targets]
        text = pane_alert_text(incident, now)
        incident["alerted_at"] = now
        incident["told"] = [target for target, _ in targets]
        state.commit(options)
        for target, why in targets:
            if options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text):
                log(f"  -> {target} ({why})")
        alerts += 1
        log(f"pane {pane['pane_id']} {where}: ALERT sent to {len(targets)} sessions")
        if options.print_alert:
            print(text)

    for key in list(incidents):
        if key not in seen_keys:
            clear(key, incidents[key], "the pane no longer exists (closed, or its tmux server restarted)")
    for key in list(memory):
        if key not in seen_keys:
            memory.pop(key)
    state.commit(options)
    return agents, at_shell, alerts, clears



def run_once(options: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    started = time.time()
    envoy = Envoy(timeout=options.http_timeout)
    state = State(options.state)
    if not state.locked:
        log(f"another run holds {options.state}; skipping this period")
        return 0
    roles_state: dict = state.data.setdefault("roles", {})
    # Incident memory, keyed by role AND holder so a role that is re-claimed by a new session
    # starts a new incident. It lives in this one file, which the service owns, and is written
    # and flushed to disk BEFORE any message goes out, so a crash between the two can never
    # resend a first alert.
    incidents: dict = state.data.setdefault("incidents", {})
    host = socket.gethostname()

    try:
        sessions = envoy.sessions()
    except RuntimeError as error:
        log(f"envoy unreachable: {error}")
        return 2
    live = {
        session["session_id"]: session for session in sessions if session.get("session_id")
    }
    local = [
        session
        for session in sessions
        if (session.get("host") or session.get("machine_id")) == host and session.get("roles")
    ]
    held = omp_sessions_by_pid()
    # Where this watch last saw each session running, for the alert's "look at the pane" step.
    pane_of: dict[str, str] = {}
    for pane_key, remembered in (state.data.get("panes") or {}).items():
        if isinstance(remembered, dict) and remembered.get("session"):
            pane_of[remembered["session"]] = (
                f"{pane_key.rsplit(':', 1)[-1]} ({remembered.get('where') or '?'},"
                f" {remembered.get('cwd') or '?'})"
            )

    # Learn the roles this machine holds, and keep what an earlier run learnt: a holder whose
    # session registration has already aged out is exactly the case this watch must still see.
    for session in local:
        for role in session.get("roles") or []:
            record = roles_state.setdefault(role, {})
            previous = record.get("holder")
            if previous != session["session_id"]:
                record.clear()
                record["holder"] = session["session_id"]
                # The old holder's incident, if any, is over: the role has moved.
                incidents.pop(f"{role}|{previous}", None)
            record.update(
                {
                    "dir": session.get("dir") or "",
                    "title": session.get("title") or "",
                    "host": host,
                    "topics": session.get("topics") or [],
                }
            )

    alerts = clears = 0
    for role in sorted(roles_state):
        record = roles_state[role]
        holder = record.get("holder")
        if not holder or record.get("host") != host:
            continue
        key = f"{role}|{holder}"
        incident = incidents.get(key)
        evidence = holder_evidence(envoy, holder, held, live, options.write_grace, started)
        pid = evidence["pid"]

        # A role whose holder another live session has taken is not this holder's incident.
        taken_by = next(
            (
                session["session_id"]
                for session in sessions
                if session.get("session_id") != holder and role in (session.get("roles") or [])
            ),
            None,
        )

        # Disagreement between the process and the registry is a registry fault, never an alert:
        # under load Envoy drops sessions that are running (envoy_sessions omits them,
        # envoy_send answers "no live session"), which the Rocq speed lane recorded on
        # 2026-10-25 15:10-15:16Z and agent infra measured again at load 233 on 2026-10-29.
        if pid and evidence["registry"] != "live":
            log(
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder} in Envoy's registry, but pid {pid} holds"
                " it -- registry fault, not an incident; no role lookup and no alert"
            )

        cause = ""
        if evidence["alive"]:
            if pid:
                record["last_proc_pid"] = pid
                record["last_proc_seen"] = started
            cause = f"this watch can see the holder again -- {evidence['found'] or 'a source'}"
        elif taken_by:
            cause = f"a new holder took the role: session {taken_by} now lists {role}"

        if cause:
            if incident and send_clear(envoy, options, role, incident, holder, cause, started):
                clears += 1
                state.commit(options)
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            if evidence["alive"]:
                log(f"{role}: ok (holder {holder}, {evidence['found']})")
            else:
                log(f"{role}: forgetting {holder} ({cause})")
                roles_state.pop(role, None)
            continue

        incident = incidents.setdefault(key, {})
        incident.setdefault("opened_at", started)
        incident["sources"] = [list(source) for source in evidence["sources"]]
        incident["verdict"] = evidence["verdict"]
        if "last_seen_alive" not in incident:
            # One deterministic value, read once when the incident opens: st_mtime of the
            # holder's transcript. Never the run's own clock -- two runs that open the same
            # incident at different seconds would disagree about when it was last written.
            incident["last_seen_alive"] = evidence["last_write"]
            incident["last_proc_pid"] = record.get("last_proc_pid")
            incident["last_pid_seen_at"] = record.get("last_proc_seen")
        unseen_for = started - incident["opened_at"]

        if unseen_for < options.grace:
            log(
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder} for {int(unseen_for)} s"
                f" (< {options.grace} s grace), holding"
            )
            state.commit(options)
            continue

        if not incident.get("alerted_at"):
            incident["transcript"] = evidence["transcript"]
            incident["handover"] = handover_files(holder, record.get("dir") or "")
            incident["listener"] = envoy.url
            incident["pane"] = pane_of.get(holder, "")
            targets, topics, notes = recipients(role, record, sessions, holder)
            incident["notes"] = notes
            for field in ("dir", "title", "host"):
                incident[field] = record.get(field)
            text = alert_text(role, incident, holder, started, "first alert")
            # On disk before a single message leaves: a resent first alert is worse than a late one.
            incident["alerted_at"] = started
            incident["told"] = [target for target, _ in targets]
            state.commit(options)
            told = []
            for target, why in targets:
                if options.dry_run or envoy.send(target, text):
                    told.append(target)
                    log(f"  -> {target} ({why})")
            for topic in topics:
                headline = text.splitlines()[0] + (
                    f" Peers on {topic}: a message to this role may reach nobody."
                )
                if not options.dry_run:
                    envoy.publish(topic, guard_verdict(headline))
                log(f"  -> topic {topic}")
            incident["told"] = told or incident["told"]
            alerts += 1
            log(f"{role}: ALERT sent to {len(told)} sessions and {len(topics)} topics")
            if options.print_alert:
                print(text)
        elif (
            started - incident["alerted_at"] >= options.escalate
            and not incident.get("escalated_at")
        ):
            text = alert_text(role, incident, holder, started, "STILL UNSEEN after 10 minutes")
            text += (
                f"\nNobody has cleared this since the first alert at {iso(incident['alerted_at'])}."
                " You own looking at the pane."
            )
            incident["escalated_at"] = started
            state.commit(options)
            if not options.dry_run:
                envoy.send(CHIEF_OF_STAFF, guard_verdict(text))
            log(f"{role}: ESCALATED to the chief of staff")
        elif unseen_for > FORGET_AFTER:
            log(
                f"{role}: dropping {holder} from the state file after {human(unseen_for)} unseen;"
                " it was alerted and escalated, and nothing re-reads it"
            )
            incidents.pop(key, None)
            roles_state.pop(role, None)
        else:
            log(
                f"{role}: {CANNOT_SEE_HOLDER} {holder} for {human(unseen_for)}, already alerted"
                f" at {iso(incident.get('alerted_at'))}"
            )
        state.commit(options)

    agents, at_shell, pane_alerts, pane_clears = check_panes(
        options, state, envoy, sessions, held, started
    )
    state.commit(options)
    state.close()
    log(
        f"checked {len(roles_state)} local role(s) and {agents} agent pane(s), {at_shell} at a shell,"
        f" in {time.time() - started:.2f}s; {alerts + pane_alerts} alert(s),"
        f" {clears + pane_clears} clear(s)"
    )
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    parser.add_argument("--grace", type=float, default=60.0, help="seconds every source must stay silent before a role alerts")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--write-grace",
        type=float,
        default=DEFAULT_WRITE_GRACE,
        help="seconds within which a write to a session's transcript counts as seeing it alive",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--escalate", type=float, default=600.0, help="seconds after an alert before the chief of staff is told again")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--pane-grace",
        type=float,
        default=180.0,
        help="seconds an agent tmux pane must sit at a shell with no omp before it alerts",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--state",
        type=Path,
        default=DEFAULT_STATE,
        help="the owned state file; another path is for tests and never sends",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--http-timeout", type=float, default=8.0)
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="decide and log, send nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--write-state", action="store_true", help="with --dry-run, still write the state file")
    parser.add_argument("--print-alert", action="store_true", help="print the alert text it would send")
    options = parser.parse_args()
    # The once-per-incident record lives in one file. A run on any other state file has its own
    # memory of what was already said, so it may never send: that is exactly how one incident
    # produced two "first alert" messages at 07:53:20Z and 07:55:18Z on 2026-10-27.
    if options.state != DEFAULT_STATE and not options.dry_run:
        options.dry_run = True
        log(f"--state is not {DEFAULT_STATE}: running as --dry-run, sending nothing")
    try:
        return run_once(options)
    except Exception as error:  # a crashing watch must say so in the journal, not vanish
        log(f"run failed: {type(error).__name__}: {error}")
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
